  "judgment": {
    "status": "confirmed",
    "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured MA worked solutions. q-80474: differentiating f_yy=-x²sin(xy) with respect to x gives -2x sin(xy)-x²y cos(xy). q-132961: f_xxz=-y²z[2sin(xyz)+xyz cos(xyz)], which evaluates to -π/2. q-132580: holding x=e fixed gives slope f_y(e,2)=4-e. q-132584: holding y=-3 fixed gives slope f_x(1,-3)=2/9-3=-25/9. q-132590: the slope is -2, so z-2π=-2(x+π/2), giving y=2 and z=-2x+π. q-36372: the slope is 5, so y=0 and z-4=5(x-2), or z=5x-6. q-132604: f_y(1,0)=-1, so (0,1,-1) is tangent; the selected vector (0,-1,1) is its negative and equally valid. q-71221: f_x(-1,1)=3, giving tangent vector (1,0,3). q-31862: halve row 2, then add twice the updated row 2 to row 1. q-131379: after the swap, R_1:=R_1+2R_2 gives the inverse with rows (2,1) and (1,0). q-35990: the gradient is (8x-3y,4y-3x+1), giving (7,7). q-36909: the gradient is (2x√(y+3),x²/(2√(y+3))), giving (8,1). q-127945: on the usual real differentiable domain x>0 and y>0, differentiating variable powers gives the selected gradient. q-71426: the gradient is cos(xyz)(yz,xz,xy), giving (π,-π,1). q-36911: 2x/(x²+1)=0 and 4(y-3)=0 give only (0,3). q-36970: y(1+y)=0, x(1+2y)=0, and 3z²=0 give exactly (0,0,0) and (0,-1,0). The source has intermediate authored errors: q-71426 repeats h_x in the initial gradient definition instead of h_y and h_z; q-36911 substitutes ln(x+1) for ln(x²+1) in the y-derivative setup; q-36970 writes ∇f instead of ∇h and repeats a zero j component instead of a zero k component. These errors do not change the stated final answers, so no final-answer disagreement is present.",
